{
  "id": "persinger-cook-1997-pattern-screen",
  "name": "Cook & Persinger 1997: 3-min pattern screen with sham blocks (36 min)",
  "category": "presence",
  "evidence": "single-lab",
  "origin": "Cook & Persinger 1997 (Laurentian University)",
  "uT": 1,
  "summary": "Successive 3-min blocks of different weak (1 uT) complex fields alternating with 3-min sham blocks through both temporal lobes, with the moment of any experience marked: Cook & Persinger 1997 reported that 9 of 15 volunteers signalled a sensed presence by button press this way. The paper lists no patterns, so six of the lab's later-published ones stand in.",
  "notes": "Cook & Persinger exposed people to 'successive 3-min. durations of bursts of different types' of 1-uT fields 'or sham-fields' and logged a button press at each experience; trying to focus on the presence moved it or changed its location. The patterns, their number and their order were not published. Here six documented Laurentian patterns alternate with sham blocks, starting with a sham: burst-firing (built-in accelerating burst, 230 points at 3 ms once every 4 s; point values unpublished), the exact Thomas table at 3 ms, the 'LTP' primed burst (5 trains in 1 s, repeated continuously; timing after Corradini 2014 and Delparte & Persinger 2007, values reconstructed), a 7 Hz sine on 1-ms points, the reversed Thomas table, and a 7 Hz sine on 10-ms points (the two point durations Booth 2003 found most effective). The set, the order, the alternation with sham and the use of both sides (T7/TP7, T8/TP8 for 'transcerebral') are inferences; in Roll et al. 2002, with an exceptional subject not shown to be Cook's, the fields went over the right temporoparietal lobes. Have a helper start the program and keep the phase display out of sight, and note the clock time of each experience to match with the phase list afterwards. Very small n; in Cook & Persinger 2001 presences came only in people with above-average temporal-lobe-sign scores, and French 2009 found such reports tracked those scores and not the field, so expectancy alone can produce the same marks. Independent double-blind tests found no field effect (Granqvist 2005; Gendle & McGrath 2012). Not for people with epilepsy or a seizure history, implanted devices or pregnancy; stop at once on fear, headache or distress.",
  "setting": "Blindfolded, reclining in a quiet, dark room; a helper runs the program and records the clock time of each reported experience, without knowing or revealing which block is active.",
  "schedule": "One 36-min screening session; at most once a week.",
  "targets": ["left-tpj","right-tpj","left-amygdala","right-amygdala"],
  "source": "Cook & Persinger 1997, Percept Mot Skills 85:683 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9347559/ ; Cook & Persinger 2001, Percept Mot Skills 92:447 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11361305/ ; Roll, Persinger, Webster, Tiller & Cook 2002, Int J Neurosci 112:197 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/12325407/ ; Tiller & Persinger 2002, Percept Mot Skills 94:26 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11883572/ ; Thomas, Prato, Kavaliers & Persinger, US 6,234,953 B1, Table 1 https://patents.google.com/patent/US6234953B1/en ; Buckner et al. 2015, PLoS ONE 10(4):e0124136 (849 points at 3 ms, 2.55-s cycle) https://europepmc.org/article/MED/25875081 ; Carniello 2020, PhD thesis, Laurentian University, p. 89 (230-point burst-firing file, 3-ms points) https://laurentian.scholaris.ca/items/8c92763c-cd19-4a21-b242-3b572a28ce55 ; Martin & Persinger 2004, Neurosci Lett 366:226 (burst-firing once every 4 s) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15276252/ ; Corradini 2014, MA thesis, Laurentian University (LTP primed-burst file) https://laurentian.scholaris.ca/items/1455e1f3-2f1d-47b5-93cd-23713d69e651 ; Delparte & Persinger 2007, Int J Neurosci 117:295 ('1 s pulse of 5 trains') https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17365115/ ; Booth, Koren & Persinger 2003, Percept Mot Skills 97:951 (7 Hz, 1- and 10-ms points) https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/14738362/ ; French et al. 2009, Cortex 45:619 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/18635163/ ; Granqvist et al. 2005, Neurosci Lett 379:1 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15849873/ ; Gendle & McGrath 2012, Percept Mot Skills 114:217 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22582690/",
